<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sayf al-Dawla</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">916–967</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamdanid-dynasty">Hamdanid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sayf al-Dawla was a Hamdanid prince who founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> in 945, while his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-dawla">Nasir al-Dawla</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. He waged constant war on the Byzantine frontier with early success, though Nikephoros Phokas sacked Aleppo in 962. His court attracted the poet al-Mutanabbi and the philosopher al-Farabi.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Ali ibn Abdallah on 22 June 916, he was the second son of Abdallah Abu'l-Hayja ibn Hamdan, of the Hamdanid branch of the Arab tribe of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banu-taghlib">Banu Taghlib</a>, long dominant around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosul">Mosul</a>. In 936 his elder brother al-Hasan promised him the governorship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diyar-bakr">Diyar Bakr</a> for help against the rebel governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a>, and Ali also subdued the Qaysi Bedouin around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saruj">Saruj</a>. When al-Hasan became amir al-umara in 942 with the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasir-al-dawla">Nasir al-Dawla</a>, Ali defeated Abu'l-Husayn al-Baridi at al-Mada'in and was made governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wasit">Wasit</a> with the title Sayf al-Dawla, Sword of the Dynasty.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After his brother's bid to control the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> failed, Sayf al-Dawla turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, where he contested the province with the Ikhshidids of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. After two wars they and the Abbasid caliph recognised his rule over northern Syria from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and the western Jazira from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a>. Tribal revolts troubled his realm until 955, but he kept the allegiance of the main <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bedouin">Bedouin</a> tribes.</p><p>Taking control of the frontier zone, the Thughur, in 945/6, he became the leading Arab prince facing a resurgent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>, which had annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malatya">Malatya</a> in 934. Against a far stronger enemy he raided deep into Byzantine territory and generally held the upper hand until 955. Then Nikephoros Phokas and his lieutenants broke Hamdanid power, annexing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cilicia">Cilicia</a> and briefly occupying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. His court drew the poets al-Mutanabbi and Abu Firas, the grammarian Ibn Jinni and the historian Abu al-Faraj al-Isfahani, who dedicated his Kitab al-Aghani to him; al-Mutanabbi wrote 22 major panegyrics to him in nine years. His realm, though, bore heavy taxes to pay for the army.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>From 963 hemiplegia and other illnesses confined him to a litter. He left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> to his chamberlain Qarquya and lived mostly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayyafariqin">Mayyafariqin</a>, while his nephew Hibat Allah and his lieutenant Nadja rebelled. He died on 8 February 967. By 969 his weakened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a> had lost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and the Syrian coast and become a Byzantine tributary. The historian Whittow called his power a paper tiger, but his struggle against the odds and his patronage of poets made him, for Bianquis, the embodiment of the Arab chivalrous ideal in its most tragic aspect.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sayf al-Dawla” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In a mountain pass in the Taurus Mountains, Byzantine forces under Leo Phokas the Younger ambushed and annihilated the army of <b>Sayf al-Dawla</b>, Hamdanid emir of Aleppo, whose raids had long troubled the empire.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-8">8 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 944-945 <b>Ali ibn Abi'l-Hayja</b>, known by his title Sayf al-Dawla, took Aleppo and, after a settlement with the Ikhshidids of Egypt, made northern Syria his own emirate.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-aleppo">Hamdanid Emirate of Aleppo</a></li><li><blockquote>His brother <b>Sayf al-Dawla</b> founded the Hamdanid Emirate of Aleppo, famed for its court poets such as al-Mutanabbi.</blockquote><a href="/state/hamdanid-emirate-of-mosul">Hamdanid emirate of Mosul</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/syria">Syria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=36.2,37.16&amp;year=949" aria-label="Aleppo on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5486 857 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Sayf al-Dawla"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5936 1127h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5936 1127h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=36.2,37.16&amp;year=949">What was here in 949 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
